          <h2>Why Disposable Pee Pads Are A $2 Billion Trap That Ruins Your Home</h2>
          <p>
            Dogs have over <strong>300 million scent receptors</strong>. To a dog, disposable pee pads smell like toxic bleach and synthetic plastic. That’s why they either miss the pad entirely, chew it into dangerous white fluff, or track wet urine footprints all over your rugs.
          </p>
          <p>
            And even when they do use them, you throw them into the trash bin where they stink up your entire kitchen until garbage day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pp-problem-cards">
          <div class="pp-problem-card">
            <div class="pp-problem-icon">✕</div>
            <div>
              <h4>They Leak Through & Warp Hardwood Floors</h4>
              <p>Cheap paper linings cannot contain volume. Urine seeps straight into your floorboards, leaving permanent stains and subfloor rot.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pp-problem-card">
            <div class="pp-problem-icon">✕</div>
            <div>
              <h4>Dangerous Shredding & Ingestion Risk</h4>
              <p>Curious pups tear plastic pads into wet rags, swallowing chemical absorbent beads that can cause life-threatening intestinal blockage.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pp-problem-card">
            <div class="pp-problem-icon">✕</div>
            <div>
              <h4>You Spend $80 to $100 Every Single Month</h4>
              <p>Disposable pads lock you into an endless subscription loop. Pet owners throw away over $1,200 in paper trash every year.</p>
